From: GoProSlowYo <68455785+goproslowyo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:25:10 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 09/15] dndcp: Copy and paste files in a Wayland session

The Wayland backend copies text, RTF and images, but not files. It now
sends a guest file copy to the host and pastes a host file copy in the
guest.

A file selection is checked first, before an image or text, because it
also advertises a text rendering of the paths. The backend reads
text/uri-list, or x-special/gnome-copied-files where a selection offers
only that. It parses each entry on its own and skips one that names no
local file, such as a trash: URI, so that the rest of the list still
goes to the host. Lists are compared after parsing, so the same list in
another rendering reads as unchanged.

A path is refused when it is relative, has a . or .. component, or has
no usable last component, which the host names its copy after. Paths
inside the vmblock mount are skipped, because a lookup there blocks
until the main loop lifts the block, and the host has no reason to pull
its own files back. For that the UI now gets the backend's vmblock
control.

The host shows the total size of the files. The paths are sized on a
separate thread, without following symlinks, and the main loop waits for
it only until the request's deadline. A walk still running then is told
to stop, and the list goes to the host with the part of the size that
was counted. At most one walk runs at a time.

A host file paste publishes URIs under the vmblock mount at once, and
the transfer starts only when an application opens one of them, as in
the X11 backend. The backend brings up vmblock with the descriptor that
vmware-user-suid-wrapper passes. A monitor thread waits on the vmblock
notify path of the staging directory and reports the open to the main
loop, which asks the host for the files. The main loop owns the block,
the staging directory and the RPC channel, so the thread only reports,
with the generation of the block it watched, and a stale report is
dropped. Without vmblock the paste is declined, since copying every host
file selection into the guest up front would cost more than it saves. A
new host clip removes the block of the previous one. Otherwise the
staging directory, which is reused while it is empty, could not be
blocked again.

Every name from the host passes LocalIsSafeStagingName(), and each URI
escapes every byte a receiver could read as syntax. A file paste is
offered as text/uri-list, x-special/gnome-copied-files and
application/x-kde-cutselection. With only a text/uri-list, Thunar keeps
Paste greyed out, because nothing in the list says whether the selection
is a copy or a cut. The URIs carry the file:// authority. The backend
remembers the list it published, so that the next host request does not
take it for a guest copy and size paths that are still blocked.

When the host sends file contents rather than paths,
LocalPrepareFileContents() writes them to a staging directory, and the
paths are published the same way without vmblock, since the data has
already arrived. The backend now advertises every copy and paste format.

The host sends its clipboard again each time the guest gets the focus.
A host file clip stays on the selection while its files can still be
pasted. Installing it again would stage the files anew and remove the
ones a paste in progress is reading. Once a transfer has failed, or the
staged files are gone, the next copy of the same files is installed
afresh.

Tested by hand with the whole series on Bazzite 44 (Fedora 44), against
VMware Workstation Pro 26H1u1, build 26.0.1.25688693, on Windows 10 Pro
22H2. On KDE Plasma 6.7.5 text, PNG, RTF and files went both ways. On
sway 1.11 file pastes into Thunar and PCManFM, host text, and a guest to
host file copy were tested. On GNOME 50.3 (Bluefin 44) text and files
went both ways, and a host image arrived as a file, through the X
selection in both directions. Only the GTK4 configuration was built.
